Excalibur Orders API – Project Presentation

SPEAKER SCRIPT:
------------------

- Opening

“Today I’ll walk you through a microservice I built using FastAPI and Docker, focusing on both backend development and DevOps practices.”

- Objective

“The goal was to build a clean API for managing orders while applying real-world DevOps concepts like containerization and version control.”

- Tech Stack

“I used Python with FastAPI for performance, Pydantic for validation, Docker for containerization, and GitHub for version control.”

- Architecture

“The architecture is simple: a client sends requests to the FastAPI service, which processes data and returns responses.
Right now, I’m using in-memory storage to keep it lightweight.”

- API Design

“I implemented core REST endpoints:

create orders
retrieve orders
fetch a single order
delete orders”
- Data Validation

“I used Pydantic schemas to enforce structure and ensure clean data.
This also automatically generates API documentation.”

- Features

“One key feature is Swagger UI, which allows easy testing and visibility into the API.
I also added error handling for cases like missing orders.”

- Docker

“I containerized the application using Docker.
This ensures consistency across environments and makes deployment much easier.”

- Testing

“I tested the API using Swagger UI and verified all CRUD operations.”

 GitHub

“I used Git for version control and pushed the project to GitHub to make it accessible and track changes.”

 Challenges

“I encountered a few issues:

GitHub authentication → solved using a Personal Access Token
Dockerfile placement → corrected project structure
Data serialization → solved using schemas”
- DevOps Thinking

“This project demonstrates:

containerization
clean API design
environment consistency
version control workflows”
- Future Improvements

“If I extend this project, I would:

integrate a database
add authentication
build a CI/CD pipeline
deploy to Kubernetes”
 Closing

“This project shows my ability to build and containerize backend services while applying DevOps principles in a practical way.”
